#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Drain the test queue: every queued test run once, in the heavy lane, failures sorted into brain.

usage: test-phase [PLAN] [--cycle C] [--lane L] [--stall S] [--fail-stall S] [--isolation-over N] [--run-dir D]
       [--project NAME] [--branch B]
       test-phase --doctor [DIR]
       test-phase --self-test

The cycle this serves: code everything with builds only, queue the test runs, drain the queue once,
read `brain list --open`, fix, run again. Nobody waits in a loop: `brain queue add` returns at once,
and one drainer (start it as a background task) takes the oldest ready job, runs it, and repeats until
nothing is ready. A second test-phase while one is draining says so and exits 0; the running drainer
picks up whatever was enqueued. A job whose --after job failed or was cancelled is cancelled with the
reason. Every command goes through tools/heavy.sh in one lane, so sessions on one machine queue instead
of loading the database together; ask whether the lane is free with `heavy.sh status dotnet`.

PLAN is optional. When given, its lines are enqueued first. One project per line, blank lines and lines
starting with # are skipped:

    <repo-dir> <test project path> [fast-filter] [args=EXTRA] [again=REASON]

The project path is relative to repo-dir. Quote a field that holds spaces. A fast filter is a class
name or a fragment of one (for MTP the fast tier passes *fragment* to --filter-class, and a rerun passes
the exact class); with it the line becomes two jobs, the fast slice and then the whole project.

Extra dotnet arguments, such as MSBuild properties, go on every command of a job: set TEST_PHASE_DOTNET_ARGS
(split like a shell line) for all jobs, and "args=EXTRA" on a plan line or `brain queue add --args=EXTRA` for
one job. They come after the project and before the logger, or before `--` in MTP mode. Example for the
Mac Catalyst desktop tests, which need Xcode checks off:

    TEST_PHASE_DOTNET_ARGS="-p:ValidateXcodeVersion=false" test-phase plan.txt
    ~/app tests/Desktop.Tests/Desktop.Tests.csproj "args=-p:ValidateXcodeVersion=false"

For each job: run it, list the failing tests, run each failing class alone, and label the test flaky if
it passes alone (exit 0, or passed tests and the name absent) or real if it fails again. It is unverified
when the rerun was cut off or failed without naming the test or passing anything. Real and unverified
go to brain as test-fail, flaky as flaky. A job that fails without naming a failing test (a build error)
goes in as test-fail too. A stall is only a note, because the watchdog says it is likely load, not code;
the note carries the passed and failed counts and the last test seen.

A stalled .NET run is first asked who hung. Local runs get a hang timeout 60 s under --stall (half of it when
--stall is short), so the test host names its running tests and writes a small dump before heavy.sh stops
the run: `--blame-hang-timeout` with a mini dump on VSTest, `--hangdump --hangdump-timeout` on MTP when the
test project references Microsoft.Testing.Extensions.HangDump. The dump goes under the run's log directory
and is deleted once the names are read. Each named test becomes a test-fail item tagged hang with a body
that says it hung; a test already open as a hang on the branch gets the tag hang-repeat too, because
hanging on two runs is not load. When no name is found the stalled note stays and says why: no package,
TEST_PHASE_HANGDUMP=off, remote lane, or a dump with no names in it.

A job holds its lane from its first command to its last, so its alone reruns are not queued behind another
session. A command that ends by a signal (exit 128 + n) is recorded as killed: no test-fail or flaky item is
filed from it, and the job goes back to the queue once; a second kill fails it. A run that adds only
failures, no passes, for --fail-stall seconds is stopped and recorded as stalled-failing with the counts.

When more than --isolation-over tests (default 10) fail in one run and every one passes alone, one
isolation item lists them and no flaky items are filed: that is a leak between tests. In a full run, a test
that fails and passes alone again on a branch that already has an open flaky item for it becomes a
test-fail tagged order-dependent. A passing full run runs `brain flaky-clear`, which closes flaky items
with no new occurrence in 5 passing full runs, resolution flaky-cleared.
Logs are under the run directory: ${LEAN_TEST_PHASE_DIR:-~/.local/share/lean/test-phase}/<timestamp>.

  --cycle      cycle label for a plan's jobs, runs and items (default: the run directory's name)
  --lane       heavy.sh lane (default dotnet)
  --stall      seconds without test progress before a command is stopped (default 600, 0 is off);
               it starts when the command starts, so keep it above the build time. A local .NET run
               also gets a hang timeout of stall minus 60 s (half of it when --stall is short) unless
               TEST_PHASE_HANGDUMP=off; the project is built first so both clocks start together
  --fail-stall seconds of failures with no test passing before a command is stopped as stalled-failing
               (default 1200, 0 is off; TEST_PHASE_FAIL_STALL)
  --isolation-over  how many tests may fail together and pass alone before they are one isolation item (10)
  --project    brain project name for a plan's jobs (default: the main repository's name, so worktrees share it)
  --branch     brain branch (default: the repo's current branch)

Every command is also recorded as a row in brain's runs table (tier fast, full or rerun, duration, time
spent waiting for the lane, load average at start and end, passed, failed, skipped, stalled, exit code),
and each item points at the run that showed it. `brain stats` reads them.

Prerequisites. A repository may list what its tests need in .lean/test-needs (Docker reachable, a path
such as a sibling checkout (looked for from the main checkout), an environment variable set, a command on PATH, optionally GNU or a minimum
version; see lib/test_needs.py). Before a local job starts, test-phase checks them and runs
check-runtime.sh. If anything is missing the job fails with the missing piece named, one blocker goes to
brain, and no test runs, so no test-fail item is filed. Values of environment variables are never printed.
`test-phase --doctor [DIR]` runs the same checks alone (DIR defaults to the repository root) and exits 1 when
something is missing. Remote lanes skip the check, since the files and variables it reads are this machine's.

When a run's failures mostly share one machine cause (DockerUnavailableException, the Testcontainers
ResourceReaper failing to start, Npgsql "Failed to connect to"; added together, at least 3 and more than half), they are not
filed as tests: one blocker for the run names the cause and the count, and those tests are not rerun, so
none of them can become a flaky item. Other failures are filed unverified, not rerun. A remote lane rests
afterwards, and a later passing full run closes the blocker.
A failed run that names no test but whose log shows a npm login error (E401, ENEEDAUTH, "Unable to authenticate")
gets the same kind of blocker, with the hint to run it on the local lane.

Remote lanes. When ~/.config/lean/remotes.ini names SSH hosts, the one drainer runs one lane per host next
to the local lane, each claiming the next ready job with `brain queue next`. A remote lane copies the job's
worktree to the host, runs the same test command in a capped container there, and streams the output back
into the same log, so parsing and brain stay local. A job that targets an Apple platform, or an npm job whose .npmrc names a private registry, runs only on the
local lane. If the host, the connection or the copy fails, the job goes back to queued and the lane rests
(TEST_PHASE_REST seconds, default 600) while the other lanes go on. TEST_PHASE_REMOTES=off turns every
remote lane off. See docs/remote-lane.md.

TEST_PHASE_RUNNER=mtp or vstest overrides detection. The wait for a busy lane is HEAVY_WAIT, here
defaulting to 3600 seconds. Exit 0 when nothing real failed and no job was cancelled during the drain, 1 otherwise. A job whose
failures were all flaky finishes done, so the jobs after it still run.

Runner support is a class with mode, command, failures, counts and class_of; another runner is another
class in RUNNERS. Dotnet runs a test project. Vitest runs a job whose test path ends in package.json (for
example web/package.json): in that folder it installs with the nearest lockfile, looked up from that folder
to the repo root (pnpm-lock.yaml: pnpm install --frozen-lockfile; package-lock.json: npm ci in the lockfile's
folder; yarn.lock is refused, and with no lockfile pnpm install runs), then runs `vitest run --reporter=verbose` (or `npm test -- --reporter=verbose`, `pnpm run test`, when the package.json has a "test" script)
(pnpm exec, or npx for npm), the fast filter pieces as file filters. A failed test file is the class, so the
rerun is per file. Playwright runs a job whose test path ends in playwright.config.ts, .js, .mjs or .cjs: the
same install in the config's folder, then `playwright test --reporter=list` with the filter pieces as file
filters. A failed test is named file > title, and the spec file is the class. The runner is chosen per job
from the test path (runner_for). TEST_PHASE_DOTNET_ARGS does not reach a Vitest or Playwright job; `args=` on
the job does. A remote lane runs them in the node_image container, which has no Playwright browsers; see
docs/remote-lane.md.
"""
